00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:15.490
This lecture is all about
distributed consensus.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:18.650
Distributed consensus is
a really important topic

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:21.100
to learn about, if you want to understand

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.560
the ins and outs of blockchain.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.350
So let's directly jump into it.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:27.960
And what I would like to start with

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.780
is to contrast once
more centralized systems

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:34.533
with decentralized
systems like blockchains.

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:39.160
A centralized system is a
system where every participant

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:42.200
in the system is linked with each other

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.530
by some kind of central instance.

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:47.390
An example would be a bank.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:50.430
So think about how you
interact with a bank.

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:53.040
You can go to a bank as a simple person

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.810
and open a bank account.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:58.940
You can facilitate transactions
through the bank by going

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:02.570
to the bank and submitting
a transaction and so on.

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:04.860
And what's your characteristic
is that the bank

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.240
is in the middle of
everything that happens

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:11.150
and can decide whether a
transaction is valid or not.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:14.940
If there's some kind of dispute
that one participant says

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:19.020
or claims that the transaction
that he got was not valid,

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:20.300
then the bank steps into it,

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:23.080
investigates it and make
some kind of resolution

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.583
to the dispute that was filed.

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:27.970
A decentralized system, like bitcoin

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:30.540
or any kind of blockchain
works differently.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.790
There is no such thing as
a centralized institution

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:37.420
that decides which
transactions are valid or not,

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:42.210
who is right, how the system
is operated, and so on.

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:44.430
And this begs a significant number

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:47.230
of very important questions
that need to be answered

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:51.830
when designing a distributed
system like a blockchain.

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:56.550
One of these questions is who
runs the distributed ledger?

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:59.340
We already learned that
a blockchain is one way

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:01.870
of distributing a ledger
through large number

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:03.770
of individual nodes.

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:05.960
But who's responsible for making sure

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:08.100
that the ledger is up to date

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.620
with all the different nodes out there?

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.140
The second question would be,

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:15.310
who decides which transactions are valid?

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:18.010
So now imagine the case
of a cryptocurrency.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:21.260
And you submit a
transaction to the network

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.820
in which you claim that
somebody else transferred you

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:26.640
a significant amount of coins.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.430
And especially, if this
claim is fraudulent,

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:32.510
how does the network make
sure that you are caught

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:35.590
and that your transaction
that you filed gets rejected

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:36.423
as invalid?

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.440
And in the case of crypto
currencies, of course,

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.390
the question that is up in the air is,

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:45.030
who can create new coins?

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.520
And then the last question,

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:50.680
how are all these kind of
meter level rules decided?

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:54.580
Who decides and who can change
these rules of the system

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:57.610
that determine the
answers to these questions

00:03:57.610 --> 00:03:59.053
that we just formulated.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:02.800
And the answer is consensus.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.250
There needs to be
something in place called

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:09.570
a consensus mechanism
that facilitates consensus

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:11.550
between the individual nodes

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:15.350
and kind of it's a system
of rules that decides

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:17.600
who is right and what happens

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.740
if somebody wants to do
something in the network.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:23.190
And something like that is just required

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:25.970
to get productive distributed system.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:28.420
Because of course, it
will rarely be the case

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:30.630
in a very large distributed system,

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:33.970
that every single one
agrees with something.

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:36.710
There will be a dispute
and a consensus mechanism

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:39.633
is the way to resolve this dispute.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:46.990
There are certain number of
different consensus mechanisms

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:49.260
out there in the world of blockchain

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:52.560
that are used by different
types of blockchains.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:53.860
And what I want to do now

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:56.600
is I want to kind of give
you a glimpse overview,

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:59.640
a quick overview or
the most important ones

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:02.400
and then go into detail
in the next lectures.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.400
First, on proof of work.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.370
And second, of proof of stake,

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:10.220
because these two mechanisms
are probably the most important

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:12.583
and the most relevant ones up to date.

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:17.040
But in this overview, now
let's start with proof of work.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:18.600
Proof of work is probably

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.340
the most widely known consensus mechanism,

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:24.160
because it was introduced by bitcoin.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:26.900
And of course, bitcoin is
something like the godfather

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:29.890
of all block chains and
of all cryptocurrencies.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:34.370
And just kind of a
disclaimer at the beginning,

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:36.820
before we go into these next sections,

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:38.410
to really understand these things,

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:41.090
you need to get very, very technical,

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:44.990
and I will not go into that
level of technicality with you.

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:47.870
What I want to do with you
is give you the intuition

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:50.300
that helps you understand
how these things work

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:52.110
on a more qualitative level,

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:55.530
which means that I will need
to simplify a number of things,

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:58.170
so please forgive me if you
already have more knowledge

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:00.510
about that, that the things
that I'm teaching you now

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:03.510
are to a certain degree simplified.

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:05.937
So back to proof of
work, how does it work?

00:06:05.937 --> 00:06:10.250
But the basic intuition is
that you as a node can opt in

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:14.150
to contribute computing
power to the network.

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:16.663
And by doing that, you
have the network to run.

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:21.230
And then proof of work
means that those nodes

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:24.160
that contribute more computing
power to the network,

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:26.860
have more say in the network.

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:29.290
And for instance, in the case of bitcoin,

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:33.010
it works the way that if you
contribute computing power,

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:35.007
then you participate
in some kind of puzzle

00:06:35.007 --> 00:06:37.240
and some kind of cryptographic puzzle

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:38.830
that you want to solve.

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:40.897
And if you are the one
that solves this puzzle,

00:06:40.897 --> 00:06:43.130
and the more computing
power you contribute,

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:46.200
the higher your chances
are of winning the puzzle.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.440
And if you are the one
who wins the puzzle,

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:52.470
you have the right to propose
the next block of transactions

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:53.990
to the network.

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:55.960
And then the other nodes can verify that

00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:59.150
and if they believe that
your transactions are valid,

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:02.280
then the block gets
added to the blockchain.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:04.330
And then at some point in the future,

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:06.410
the next block gets proposed.

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:08.410
So the more computing power you provide,

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:11.110
the more say you have in the network.

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:13.540
And bitcoin is one popular
example using that.

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:15.380
Another one would be Litecoin.

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:17.760
And there are large number
of other coins doing that

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:18.593
as well.

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:24.680
A bit more modern alternative
to that is proof of stake.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:27.650
Instead of using kind of computing power

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:29.580
as the decisive factor,

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:33.510
the coin holding of the
individual nodes are used.

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:36.060
So the more coins you hold,

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:37.690
kind of the more of a way,

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:40.830
the more of a very rich
person you are in the network,

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:43.110
the more say you have in the network,

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:46.550
and the larger your chances
of you being the one

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:49.177
who can propose the next
transactions to the network.

00:07:49.177 --> 00:07:53.030
And you can imagine this as
some kind of lottery system,

00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:57.720
where you get more tickets,
the more coins you have.

00:07:57.720 --> 00:07:59.330
And the more tickets you have, of course,

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:02.240
the higher your chances of winning are

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:06.120
and winning means kind of
making decisions in the network.

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:09.750
And two popular examples are Lisk or Neo,

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:12.250
even though I'm aware that
for Neo it can be debated

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:15.820
whether this is actually
an arch type proof of stake

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:18.020
or maybe also different,

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:20.420
but probably that could
go too far if we dive

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:21.583
into that right now.

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:24.800
A third consensus mechanism introduced

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:29.480
by new economic movement,
NEM, is proof of information,

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:31.313
sorry, proof of importance.

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:35.660
And here, the decision
to be made, who decides,

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:37.440
who makes the decisions in the network

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:39.660
are based on the network activity

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:41.960
and the reputation within the network.

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:46.240
So simply put, the more
useful you are to the network,

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:47.970
the more say you have,

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:51.270
and your level of usefulness is determined

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:53.000
by a certain number of factors

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:56.480
that then also every blockchain
can set for themselves.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:00.350
NEM is the coin that
popularized or introduced

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:02.530
this proof of work mechanism.

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:05.390
And then there are a couple
of others like proof of burn,

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:09.030
proof of capacity, proof
of activity and so on.

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:11.010
In each one of them, every one of them

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:13.843
has distinctive advantages
and disadvantages.

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:17.110
And this is also the
point that I would like

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:18.713
to close this lecture with.

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:22.880
To date, there's no one
single consensus mechanism

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:25.853
that is generally believed
to be the very best one.

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:29.940
Every single one has
advantages and disadvantages.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:31.930
So coins make different choices.

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:34.300
Blockchains can make
different choices depending

00:09:34.300 --> 00:09:36.333
on what is important to them.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:39.830
And probably one step further,

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:43.190
probably in the future,
new consensus mechanisms

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:46.670
will arise which we haven't
been thinking about so far.

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:49.690
And then these new consensus
mechanisms might be then

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:51.930
the gold standard in the future.

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:54.940
So the field of consensus
mechanisms is a quickly

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:58.340
and steadily evolving
one kind of fighting for

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:01.100
or searching for the very
best mechanism that fulfills

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:03.900
the need of the individual
blockchain applications

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:06.090
that you want to build.

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:07.810
This lecture focuses

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:10.893
on the proof of work consensus mechanism.

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:14.090
And before we go into more detail

00:10:14.090 --> 00:10:16.350
about how proof of work works,

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:18.840
let's first recap why there is a need

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:21.610
for something like consensus mechanism.

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:24.620
The consensus mechanism
fundamentally wants to answer

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:27.470
the question, what will
be the next valid block

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:28.810
of the blockchain?

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:30.930
And if you think about it,
you have the blockchain.

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:33.960
And now there are all kinds of miners

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:36.680
and all kinds of open
transactions out there,

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:40.230
but who decides what the
next block is going to be?

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:42.510
And of course, there needs to be consensus

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:44.110
around this question.

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:47.480
Everybody in the network
needs to understand and agree

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:49.690
on what the next block needs to be.

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:52.790
So let's go into this
one level more detail

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:54.510
and just understand this issue

00:10:54.510 --> 00:10:56.890
on a bit more systematic level.

00:10:56.890 --> 00:10:59.700
So this is a stylized
version of the blockchain.

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:01.730
So just imagine already some blocks filled

00:11:01.730 --> 00:11:04.980
with transaction data that
has occurred in the past.

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:08.200
And now the next level
block needs to be selected.

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:10.380
And the block is
fundamentally nothing else

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:13.763
than a collection of
transactions that have occurred.

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:17.390
So what exactly this
means is that if you want

00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:19.570
to post the transaction to the network,

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:21.740
so let's think about the bitcoin network,

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:23.790
you want to send somebody money,

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:27.540
you post the transaction
to the bitcoin blockchain.

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:30.250
And this transaction lands
in something that is called,

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:32.550
especially in the bitcoin community,

00:11:32.550 --> 00:11:35.580
a memory pool or short, mempool.

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:38.760
So the mempool consists of all the open,

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:43.240
unconfirmed transactions posted
to the blockchain network.

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:45.850
But in order for them now to be validated,

00:11:45.850 --> 00:11:48.710
they need to be included
in the official blockchain

00:11:48.710 --> 00:11:50.050
and one of the blocks.

00:11:50.050 --> 00:11:53.700
So now a miner comes, he
picks up some transactions

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:56.540
which are currently floating
around in the mempool

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:59.470
and aggregates them into the next block

00:11:59.470 --> 00:12:04.470
and he now tries to post this
next block to the blockchain.

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:08.310
So many miners are
competing against each other

00:12:08.310 --> 00:12:11.410
in order to post one
block to the blockchain.

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:14.710
But of course, only one
block can be the next block

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:16.293
that actually gets added to it.

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:22.160
So how do you decide which miner
is the one that gets chosen

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:24.390
to publish the next block.

00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:28.290
And in order for this proof
of work comes into play,

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:30.390
and kind of to put it shortly,

00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:31.590
to propose a new block,

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:35.180
a miner need to show something
that is called Nonce.

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:38.980
A Nonce value that together
with all other block inputs

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:43.210
produces a hash value within
a certain valid range.

00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:46.540
So this is something that is
also called a hash puzzle.

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:49.380
Different miners compete with each other

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:51.980
in order to solve the hash puzzle.

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:55.690
And the miner that first
solves this hash puzzle

00:12:55.690 --> 00:12:59.340
is the one that can post
the block to the blockchain.

00:13:01.410 --> 00:13:02.880
To understand this a bit further,

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:04.510
so what is the hash puzzle?

00:13:04.510 --> 00:13:06.010
So just the visualization here,

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:09.770
imagine this is the
complete range of values

00:13:09.770 --> 00:13:11.720
that a hash function can take.

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:13.960
So remember back at the
introductory section

00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:16.750
where we introduced
what hash functions are,

00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:19.850
hash functions are functions that take

00:13:19.850 --> 00:13:22.740
whatever input you have, you
can put any input into it,

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:24.990
and then they give you a certain output

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:27.730
for the input value that you inserted.

00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:31.400
So measuring this range of
values to be the complete range

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:34.910
of values that the hash function
can give you as an output.

00:13:34.910 --> 00:13:39.460
Then the algorithm defines
that only a certain part

00:13:39.460 --> 00:13:42.480
of that hash function
would be considered valid.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:45.540
So this means that often it's done the way

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:47.530
that there's a threshold,
a value threshold,

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:51.160
so you need to come up with a hash output

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:53.780
that has kind of a number or certain value

00:13:53.780 --> 00:13:57.970
below a certain threshold
that is being defined.

00:13:57.970 --> 00:14:02.810
Now also, again, think back
at the stylized blockchain

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:04.110
that we talked about earlier

00:14:04.110 --> 00:14:05.730
where we saw the different blocks.

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:07.370
And we changed the values

00:14:07.370 --> 00:14:11.270
and saw how the different
hash functions concatenated

00:14:11.270 --> 00:14:13.290
the individual blocks to one blockchain.

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:16.930
We saw that there was one
value that was called Nonce.

00:14:16.930 --> 00:14:18.000
And it's called Nonce,

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:20.270
it stands for nonsense,

00:14:20.270 --> 00:14:21.610
this is barely what it means.

00:14:21.610 --> 00:14:24.030
So this has no intrinsic value,

00:14:24.030 --> 00:14:28.140
except for the miners
needing to find the Nonce,

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:32.380
that together with all the
other content of a block

00:14:32.380 --> 00:14:33.950
that they want to propose,

00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:36.430
if all this content,
including the Nonce is put

00:14:36.430 --> 00:14:37.820
into a hash function,

00:14:37.820 --> 00:14:42.320
the output of this hash
function fulfills the criterion

00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:44.410
of validity that is given here,

00:14:44.410 --> 00:14:47.470
or kind of has a value
that is considered valid

00:14:47.470 --> 00:14:48.800
by the network.

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:52.510
And this is basically done by
nothing else, the brute force.

00:14:52.510 --> 00:14:55.150
The miner needs to try
lots and lots and lots

00:14:55.150 --> 00:14:58.280
of different Nonce values
in order to come up with

00:14:58.280 --> 00:15:02.114
the kind of total hash
output that is valid.

00:15:02.114 --> 00:15:07.114
And the smaller the range
of valid hash outcomes is,

00:15:07.850 --> 00:15:12.850
of course, the more difficult
it is for the miner to come up

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:16.163
or to find the Nonce value
that makes the equation valid.

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:20.560
So, kind of in a bit more
systematic way or technical way,

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:22.280
what is the proof of work?

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:24.970
Proof of work is a piece of data.

00:15:24.970 --> 00:15:26.320
So, something like the Nonce

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:28.390
or maybe the Nonce combined
with the other inputs

00:15:28.390 --> 00:15:31.300
that then give a hash function output

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:34.890
that is costly and time
consuming to produce.

00:15:34.890 --> 00:15:35.960
In order to find that,

00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:39.140
you will need to brute force
try lots and lots and lots

00:15:39.140 --> 00:15:41.420
of different Nonce values
in order to come up

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:43.170
with the value that is valid.

00:15:43.170 --> 00:15:47.290
And it needs to be easy
to verify the validity

00:15:47.290 --> 00:15:49.260
of the value by others.

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:51.790
So if a miner now believes
that he has found the solution

00:15:51.790 --> 00:15:56.470
and posts this block with his
Nonce value to the blockchain,

00:15:56.470 --> 00:15:59.830
it needs to be easy for the
other nodes in the network

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:02.310
to understand that this,
yes, this is a valid block.

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:03.750
And because it's valid,

00:16:03.750 --> 00:16:07.100
it is the next block of the blockchain.

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:11.240
So this is how proof of work works, right?

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:14.270
All the miners try to
solve the hash puzzle

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:15.780
of finding the Nonce that together

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:18.460
with the other transaction
data gives them a valid block.

00:16:18.460 --> 00:16:20.980
And the very first miner who comes up

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:22.730
with such a valid block

00:16:22.730 --> 00:16:25.920
is then the one who can
kind of post his block

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:28.520
to the network and then
is selected by the others

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:30.460
as the next valid block.

00:16:30.460 --> 00:16:32.750
Now, of course, the question
that is in the air is,

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:34.480
why would people even do that?

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:36.690
Why would people compete in such a way

00:16:36.690 --> 00:16:38.920
and invest all that computing power,

00:16:38.920 --> 00:16:41.770
which is quite a significant
amount of computing power,

00:16:41.770 --> 00:16:45.380
in order to solve a block
and be chosen as the one

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:46.960
who composed this block to the network.

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:50.650
And this is due to the
introduction of incentives.

00:16:50.650 --> 00:16:53.650
So pretty much every
blockchain system has some kind

00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:56.800
of an incentive system
in order for miners,

00:16:56.800 --> 00:17:00.620
which are basically the
nodes to engage in the system

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:03.020
and basically run the system.

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:05.070
And now very specifically,

00:17:05.070 --> 00:17:06.830
especially for the bitcoin network,

00:17:06.830 --> 00:17:09.200
the rewards are twofold.

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:10.990
The first kind of reward is something that

00:17:10.990 --> 00:17:13.050
is called a block reward.

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:16.410
So the miner that is
chosen as being the one

00:17:16.410 --> 00:17:20.370
who can post this block
to network gets a reward.

00:17:20.370 --> 00:17:22.540
And this is quite a significant reward.

00:17:22.540 --> 00:17:26.620
We'll later go into the details
for bitcoin in a moment.

00:17:26.620 --> 00:17:28.340
So this is the block reward.

00:17:28.340 --> 00:17:30.860
The second type of reward is something

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:32.570
like a transaction fee.

00:17:32.570 --> 00:17:35.260
So if you are a user and
you post your transaction

00:17:35.260 --> 00:17:37.826
to the memory pool, to the mempool,

00:17:37.826 --> 00:17:39.480
which is the pool from which the miners

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:41.100
then pick up the transactions

00:17:41.100 --> 00:17:43.220
and combine them into one block,

00:17:43.220 --> 00:17:45.740
you need to pay in
transaction fee for the miner

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:47.580
to pick your transaction.

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:51.240
And this works more or less
like an open market place.

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:55.270
The miner can decide by himself
which transactions he want

00:17:55.270 --> 00:17:57.320
to pick up and combine them in one block

00:17:57.320 --> 00:17:59.260
and of course, the rational miner

00:17:59.260 --> 00:18:02.530
will pick up those transactions first,

00:18:02.530 --> 00:18:05.410
that gives him the
highest transaction fees.

00:18:05.410 --> 00:18:09.000
So as a user, you can set
your own transaction fees.

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:12.030
And of course, the higher,
the faster your transaction

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:14.690
will be processed by the network.

00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:19.020
And we will see in a later
lecture of this whole course,

00:18:19.020 --> 00:18:21.303
how transactions fees evolved over time.

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:26.570
Now, let's dive into some specifics

00:18:26.570 --> 00:18:28.860
of the bitcoin blockchain
because, of course,

00:18:28.860 --> 00:18:33.240
bitcoin is the most prominent
user of proof of work.

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:37.580
So bitcoin does run proof of
work as in consensus mechanism,

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:39.200
and it is set up in a way

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:42.700
that the so called block
time amounts to 10 minutes.

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:46.520
The block time is the time
that the network takes

00:18:46.520 --> 00:18:47.890
between adding a new block.

00:18:47.890 --> 00:18:50.460
So every 10 minutes on average,

00:18:50.460 --> 00:18:53.870
a new block is added to the network.

00:18:53.870 --> 00:18:58.470
The block reward we talked
about earlier is 12.5 bitcoins.

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:02.850
So the miner who is chosen
as the one who kind of solves

00:19:02.850 --> 00:19:05.360
the hash puzzle and then picks
the next block to be added

00:19:05.360 --> 00:19:09.440
to the blockchain receives 12.5 bitcoin.

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:13.570
And this is, of course, a
very significant reward.

00:19:13.570 --> 00:19:16.260
At the time of me recording this video,

00:19:16.260 --> 00:19:20.380
one bitcoin fluctuates at around $10,000,

00:19:20.380 --> 00:19:23.367
so 12.5 bitcoin will roughly be $120,000,

00:19:25.850 --> 00:19:28.730
which is a very significant amount.

00:19:28.730 --> 00:19:31.650
Another important piece of
information that you need

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:33.543
to be aware of is that for bitcoin,

00:19:34.413 --> 00:19:39.330
the block reward halves
every 210,000 blocks.

00:19:39.330 --> 00:19:41.500
And as one block takes about 10 minutes,

00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:44.300
this is approximately four years.

00:19:44.300 --> 00:19:46.817
So in the very beginning,
the block reward for

00:19:46.817 --> 00:19:51.440
and finding the next
block was 50 bitcoins.

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:55.840
And since then, it is halving
approximately every four year.

00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:59.963
This is just one specificity
of the bitcoin network.

00:20:01.080 --> 00:20:03.860
And then another thing to
keep in mind on bitcoin

00:20:03.860 --> 00:20:07.640
is that bitcoin
recalibrates the difficulty

00:20:07.640 --> 00:20:11.410
of the hash puzzle it is
presenting to the miners.

00:20:11.410 --> 00:20:15.580
So of course, the more
computing power is introduced

00:20:15.580 --> 00:20:17.410
by miners to the network,

00:20:17.410 --> 00:20:20.990
the quicker it would be in
order to solve the hash puzzle,

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:25.180
because more computers now can
in parallel with brute force,

00:20:25.180 --> 00:20:27.770
try out different Nonce
values in order to come up

00:20:27.770 --> 00:20:28.750
with a valid one.

00:20:28.750 --> 00:20:30.670
And the more computing power you have,

00:20:30.670 --> 00:20:33.943
the faster you will be
able to solve the puzzle.

00:20:34.940 --> 00:20:37.790
And for this, this is
necessary to recalibrate

00:20:37.790 --> 00:20:39.700
the difficulty of the hash puzzle

00:20:39.700 --> 00:20:44.280
if you want to have the stable
block time of 10 minutes

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:45.690
for every block.

00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:49.760
And in the bitcoin
network, every 2016 blocks,

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:53.120
which is approximately
14 days, so two weeks,

00:20:53.120 --> 00:20:57.400
the bitcoin network recalibrates
the block difficulty

00:20:57.400 --> 00:21:00.720
in order to make sure that the
block time always fluctuates

00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:03.250
around 10 minutes.

00:21:03.250 --> 00:21:07.560
This lecture focuses on an
alternative to proof of work.

00:21:07.560 --> 00:21:09.550
It is proof of stake,

00:21:09.550 --> 00:21:13.150
another consensus
mechanism that is endorsed

00:21:13.150 --> 00:21:16.700
by some blockchains to solve the problem

00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:18.900
that we just discussed earlier.

00:21:18.900 --> 00:21:21.410
And let's just remind
us what the problem is.

00:21:21.410 --> 00:21:24.230
The problem is that there
needs to be a next block,

00:21:24.230 --> 00:21:27.180
or a race, there needs to be
a next block that is selected

00:21:27.180 --> 00:21:28.370
to the blockchain network.

00:21:28.370 --> 00:21:30.030
And there needs to be consensus

00:21:30.030 --> 00:21:32.310
among all the participating nodes,

00:21:32.310 --> 00:21:35.110
what the next block is going to be.

00:21:35.110 --> 00:21:38.360
So how do we achieve consensus about

00:21:38.360 --> 00:21:39.870
what the next block is going to be?

00:21:39.870 --> 00:21:43.373
How do we collectively
decide on the next block?

00:21:44.430 --> 00:21:46.850
And why we discussed in
detail how that works

00:21:46.850 --> 00:21:51.200
with proof of work, which
is probably the most common

00:21:51.200 --> 00:21:55.780
and the most popular consensus
mechanism at the moment.

00:21:55.780 --> 00:21:58.180
Another consensus mechanism
that is gaining more

00:21:58.180 --> 00:22:01.200
and more traction is proof of stake.

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:06.200
And probably the most popular
proponent of it is Ethereum.

00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:10.110
So while Ethereum is
at the moment still ran

00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:11.570
on proof of work,

00:22:11.570 --> 00:22:15.230
it is very close to
implementing proof of stake

00:22:15.230 --> 00:22:19.890
in the so called Casper update
in the very short feature.

00:22:19.890 --> 00:22:24.120
So it is a highly relevant
consensus mechanism.

00:22:24.120 --> 00:22:25.640
So, how does it work?

00:22:25.640 --> 00:22:27.910
And the idea is that we are going away

00:22:27.910 --> 00:22:31.350
from miners solving something
like hash puzzles needing

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:34.790
to invest huge amount of computing powers

00:22:34.790 --> 00:22:38.380
in order to find some kind
of hash function output

00:22:38.380 --> 00:22:40.990
that solves certain requirements.

00:22:40.990 --> 00:22:42.640
It goes away from that,

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:47.280
and focuses more on users
investing certain amounts

00:22:47.280 --> 00:22:48.330
of their coins,

00:22:48.330 --> 00:22:49.940
so investing some kind of stake

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:51.720
that they have in the network,

00:22:51.720 --> 00:22:55.160
in order to be able to then
be the one who has chosen

00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:57.740
to propose the next block.

00:22:57.740 --> 00:23:00.280
So in a bit more stylized way,

00:23:00.280 --> 00:23:02.410
so let's imagine these guys are miners

00:23:02.410 --> 00:23:06.320
and they want to become part
of the consensus mechanism.

00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:08.040
So in order to do that,

00:23:08.040 --> 00:23:10.930
they need to lock up a part of their funds

00:23:10.930 --> 00:23:12.940
that they have in their network

00:23:12.940 --> 00:23:16.540
in some kind of a virtual treasure locker.

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:19.360
So this means is that they're
locking these funds away

00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:23.390
and won't be able to use them
for certain amount of time.

00:23:23.390 --> 00:23:26.450
And what exactly that amount
of time is, of course,

00:23:26.450 --> 00:23:28.860
largely differs by implementation

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:30.360
of the individual blockchains.

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:34.870
So now people have committed
to the consensus mechanism.

00:23:34.870 --> 00:23:38.990
And now some kind of lottery
mechanism comes into play.

00:23:38.990 --> 00:23:43.990
And the lottery mechanism
chooses now one of the miners

00:23:44.720 --> 00:23:48.880
to be the one who can
propose the next block.

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:52.070
And it does so to a
certain extent randomly,

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:56.010
but it takes into account the size

00:23:56.010 --> 00:23:58.440
of the individual coin holdings

00:23:58.440 --> 00:24:01.810
that the miners have locked
up in order to participate

00:24:01.810 --> 00:24:04.820
in this consensus mechanism
or in this lottery.

00:24:04.820 --> 00:24:08.120
Another way to think about
it is that as a miner,

00:24:08.120 --> 00:24:10.940
you can kind of contribute
or you can buy tickets

00:24:10.940 --> 00:24:12.080
to the lottery, right?

00:24:12.080 --> 00:24:14.260
And the more tickets you buy, of course,

00:24:14.260 --> 00:24:16.630
the higher the chances are for you to win.

00:24:16.630 --> 00:24:19.550
The more money you pay,
the more tickets you get.

00:24:19.550 --> 00:24:22.760
But of course, even if you
have lots and lots of tickets,

00:24:22.760 --> 00:24:25.580
while as others maybe have
only very few tickets,

00:24:25.580 --> 00:24:28.080
there is no guarantee that in the end,

00:24:28.080 --> 00:24:30.460
you are the one who actually wins.

00:24:30.460 --> 00:24:33.380
Though the more tickets you
have, the higher your funds,

00:24:33.380 --> 00:24:35.200
the higher your stake.

00:24:35.200 --> 00:24:37.200
This is why it's called proof of stake.

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:39.890
The higher your stake,
the higher your chances

00:24:39.890 --> 00:24:43.120
to be the one that is
then in the end selected

00:24:43.120 --> 00:24:45.103
to propose the new block.

00:24:46.450 --> 00:24:48.720
And I just want to mention the caveat

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:51.620
that this is a kind of simplified view

00:24:51.620 --> 00:24:54.150
of how proof of stake works.

00:24:54.150 --> 00:24:57.330
Most implementations can
have a much more complex,

00:24:57.330 --> 00:24:58.850
the mechanics involved

00:24:58.850 --> 00:25:00.640
and it's also very noteworthy

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:03.700
that there are lots
and lots of variations,

00:25:03.700 --> 00:25:05.910
lots and lots of different implementations

00:25:05.910 --> 00:25:09.010
that are either discussed
or already implemented

00:25:09.010 --> 00:25:10.300
with some coins.

00:25:10.300 --> 00:25:12.370
So, if you talk about proof of stake,

00:25:12.370 --> 00:25:16.260
many people mean very
different things about that.

00:25:16.260 --> 00:25:18.740
But I think that this is kind
of the common denominator,

00:25:18.740 --> 00:25:22.300
the basic intuition that
you will need to understand

00:25:22.300 --> 00:25:24.783
in order to understand
what this is all about.

00:25:26.480 --> 00:25:30.960
That being said, why would
somebody need proof of stake?

00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:32.980
So what are the advantages?

00:25:32.980 --> 00:25:35.420
And one of the key advantages is that

00:25:35.420 --> 00:25:37.760
it is much more energy efficient.

00:25:37.760 --> 00:25:41.110
So many people criticize
blockchains like bitcoin

00:25:41.110 --> 00:25:43.980
for the huge energy consumption.

00:25:43.980 --> 00:25:46.590
At the moment, the whole bitcoin network

00:25:46.590 --> 00:25:49.550
is allegedly consuming more energy

00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:51.810
than some small countries.

00:25:51.810 --> 00:25:54.700
So it is an extreme amount
of energy that is consumed

00:25:54.700 --> 00:25:57.270
and the reason why it
consumes so much energy

00:25:57.270 --> 00:25:59.250
are these miner farms,

00:25:59.250 --> 00:26:02.100
which just have like the
huge amount of computers,

00:26:02.100 --> 00:26:03.453
huge computer wrecks,

00:26:04.370 --> 00:26:06.310
which just provide computing power

00:26:06.310 --> 00:26:09.770
which provide hash power in
order to solve the hash puzzles

00:26:09.770 --> 00:26:12.100
because the more
computing power you invest

00:26:12.100 --> 00:26:13.400
into the network,

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:16.880
the higher your chances
of solving the next block,

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:18.130
and the higher your chances

00:26:18.130 --> 00:26:20.470
of thereby getting the block reward,

00:26:20.470 --> 00:26:22.693
which is quite significant
as we have seen.

00:26:23.940 --> 00:26:27.250
So proof of stake doesn't
require computing power,

00:26:27.250 --> 00:26:30.580
or not at least not even close
the amount of computing power

00:26:30.580 --> 00:26:33.600
that is introduced by proof of work,

00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:36.170
which is why proof of
stake can be a solution

00:26:36.170 --> 00:26:39.070
to the sustainability problem
of blockchains like bitcoin.

00:26:40.810 --> 00:26:42.420
Another advantage is that,

00:26:42.420 --> 00:26:45.360
the block reward you need
to give out to the miners

00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:47.040
isn't that high.

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:49.520
So the reason why in the bitcoin network

00:26:49.520 --> 00:26:51.940
or other networks using proof of work,

00:26:51.940 --> 00:26:54.690
the block rewards are
so high, so significant,

00:26:54.690 --> 00:26:57.690
is because the miners
need to be compensated

00:26:57.690 --> 00:26:59.800
for the huge amount of computing power

00:26:59.800 --> 00:27:02.970
and electricity they
invest into the network.

00:27:02.970 --> 00:27:07.440
As the costs of participating
in terms of just energy

00:27:07.440 --> 00:27:10.630
and utility costs aren't
as high in proof of stake,

00:27:10.630 --> 00:27:12.780
the block rewards don't
need to be as high.

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:17.460
And finally, the centralization risk

00:27:17.460 --> 00:27:20.570
is supposed to not be as
high as proof of work.

00:27:20.570 --> 00:27:24.120
If you control huge mining
farms in proof of work,

00:27:24.120 --> 00:27:26.150
you can control the network.

00:27:26.150 --> 00:27:30.340
And then this does pose
a risk of centralization

00:27:30.340 --> 00:27:32.600
of the network, which of course,

00:27:32.600 --> 00:27:36.423
goes against the decentralized
aspect of blockchain.

00:27:37.410 --> 00:27:39.700
Of course, the counter
argument to proof of stake

00:27:39.700 --> 00:27:41.950
is that with proof of stake,

00:27:41.950 --> 00:27:44.460
the guy who owns the most coins, right,

00:27:44.460 --> 00:27:48.560
the richest guy in the
system can be now the one

00:27:48.560 --> 00:27:50.960
who gets the control over the network.

00:27:50.960 --> 00:27:54.430
So it centralizes towards
rich people towards the wealth

00:27:55.389 --> 00:27:58.680
to the our possibilities
to design proof of stake

00:27:58.680 --> 00:28:01.120
in a way that this is not taking place.

00:28:01.120 --> 00:28:05.370
So an easy way to think about
it would be to kind of cap

00:28:05.370 --> 00:28:10.370
the maximum amount of
contributions that you get

00:28:10.720 --> 00:28:14.530
by introducing high coin
values to the network.

00:28:14.530 --> 00:28:17.700
The simple solution of just
capping it has disadvantages

00:28:17.700 --> 00:28:20.580
and will be too complicated
to go into this at the moment,

00:28:20.580 --> 00:28:24.000
but just understand that
there are kind of ways

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:26.400
in order to mitigate the problem.

00:28:26.400 --> 00:28:28.400
And on the disadvantages side,

00:28:28.400 --> 00:28:30.760
there is another very well known problem,

00:28:30.760 --> 00:28:33.830
which is called nothing at stake problem.

00:28:33.830 --> 00:28:38.040
And it describes the
problem that rational miners

00:28:38.040 --> 00:28:42.020
and such proof of stake
regime might be incentivized

00:28:42.020 --> 00:28:45.110
to produce fraudulent
invalid blocks all the time,

00:28:45.110 --> 00:28:48.380
which then might congest the network

00:28:48.380 --> 00:28:50.620
and might make it impossible for the nodes

00:28:50.620 --> 00:28:52.470
to reach consensus.

00:28:52.470 --> 00:28:54.060
If you would like to know more about this,

00:28:54.060 --> 00:28:56.030
just google nothing at stake problem

00:28:56.030 --> 00:28:59.630
and you will find extensive
documentation on that.

00:28:59.630 --> 00:29:01.590
Could go into a bit more, too much detail

00:29:01.590 --> 00:29:03.000
if we cover that here,

00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:04.020
but if you Google it,

00:29:04.020 --> 00:29:07.190
you will find lots of also
easy to understand explanations

00:29:07.190 --> 00:29:10.333
about what the nothing at
stake problem is all about.

